<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>Coffee Blend — 4-Origin Supply Chain (representative test AOI)</name>
    <description>
      Illustrative EUDR test scenario for the coffee commodity (Regulation 2023/1115),
      modelling a real-world roaster blend that sources across multiple continents
      rather than a single origin. EUDR requires geolocation for every plot feeding a
      blended shipment (Article 9), so a realistic due-diligence file for a blend looks
      exactly like this: several small plots, several countries, one submission.
      Four origins, chosen to span distinct producing regions:
        - Brazil (Cerrado Mineiro, Minas Gerais) — South America, estate-scale arabica.
        - Ethiopia (Hararghe highlands, East Ethiopia) — East Africa; historically traded
          via Somali overland routes to the port of Berbera, and the well-documented
          source of the spicy/winey natural-process character ("a little pep") often
          associated with Somali/Horn-of-Africa coffee in blends. Note: Somalia itself
          has very limited modern coffee cultivation (small emerging plots in the
          Somaliland highlands); Hararghe is the defensible, well-documented origin for
          this flavor profile and is used here rather than an unverified Somalia claim.
        - Vietnam (Dak Lak, Central Highlands) — mainland Southeast Asia, robusta.
        - Indonesia (Gayo highlands, Central Aceh, Sumatra) — maritime Southeast Asia,
          smallholder arabica.
      Each plot is placed at plausible rural coordinates within the named producing
      region to exercise the pipeline against real, defensible geographies — none
      asserts the surveyed boundary of a specific named farm or claims ownership/consent
      for a real property.
    </description>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Brazil — Cerrado Mineiro estate plot</name>
      <description>Representative test AOI, ~43 ha, near Patrocinio, Minas Gerais — not a real surveyed boundary.</description>
      <Polygon>
        <outerBoundaryIs>
          <LinearRing>
            <coordinates>
              -46.992500,-18.940819,0
              -46.989702,-18.941253,0
              -46.988616,-18.943900,0
              -46.989901,-18.946359,0
              -46.992500,-18.947045,0
              -46.995395,-18.946638,0
              -46.996615,-18.943900,0
              -46.995131,-18.941412,0
              -46.992500,-18.940819,0
            </coordinates>
          </LinearRing>
        </outerBoundaryIs>
      </Polygon>
    </Placemark>
